# Accepted vulnerability risk. Read by the Trivy scans in .github/workflows/ci.yml
# and .github/workflows/image-scan.yml; the contract below is enforced by
# tests/test_trivyignore.py, not by Trivy, which accepts an entry with no expiry
# and then suppresses it forever.
#
# One finding per line, immediately preceded by a comment giving the reason:
#
#   # ubuntu has no fixed package yet; revisit at the next base-image bump
#   CVE-2026-12345 exp:2026-12-01
#
# The expiry is mandatory and must be yyyy-mm-dd. Past it, Trivy stops
# suppressing and the finding blocks the gate again — which is the point: an
# acceptance with no end date is indistinguishable from a forgotten one.
#
# Suppression is per-ID and nothing else is an accepted-risk mechanism. Lowering
# the scans' `severity`, dropping the gate step, or setting its `exit-code` to 0
# silences every finding at once and is not the same decision as accepting one.
#
# Per-ID means the ID is matched ANYWHERE in the image, not scoped to the target
# it was found in — the plain format has no `paths:` field. Before adding an entry,
# check no other target carries the same ID where it would be fixable: hiding a
# bumpable finding is the failure mode this cannot express.
#
# If the gate goes red on an ID listed below, suspect a lapsed expiry first. Trivy
# stops suppressing silently on that date and reports the finding as if it were
# new, with nothing pointing back at this file.
#
# A deb finding whose fixed version is already in the Ubuntu archive points at
# the cached apt layer instead: the CI build and the weekly scan reuse it from
# the gha cache, so they keep reporting what a fresh build would not. The
# published image is built without that cache. Changing the apt RUN in the
# Dockerfile rebuilds the layer and clears it; don't add an entry here for it.

# undici CVE-2026-109869 (DoS on an unhandled stream error); the fix needs
# 6.29.1, released upstream 2026-10-15. npm 12.2.1 (pins/npm.env) vendors
# 6.29.0; pnpm 12.17.0 (pins/pnpm.env) carries 6.29.1, so npm's copy is the
# only one left. npm 12.2.2 (published 2026-11-04) bundles 6.29.1 — verified in
# the published tarball — so the fix is out; 12.2.2 sits inside the 7-day soak
# until ~11-11 (which is why the 11-09 refresh held it) and the Monday 11-16
# refresh picks it up. Drop these entries once pins/npm.env >= 12.2.2 lands;
# the 11-25 expiry bounds that wait. If the pin has not moved by then,
# re-check the undici bundles before extending.
CVE-2026-109869 exp:2026-11-25
# undici CVE-2026-109383 (certificate validation bypass via TLS options dropped
# in Agent), flagged from 2026-10-28. Fixed in 6.29.1, and it hits the
# same npm copy of 6.29.0, so CVE-2026-109869's expiry and reason apply.
CVE-2026-109383 exp:2026-11-25
# undici CVE-2026-87018 (DoS via TLS renegotiation with a mismatched
# certificate). Same fix, copies, expiry and reason as CVE-2026-109383.
CVE-2026-87018 exp:2026-11-25
# golang.org/x/net CVE-2026-78661 (DoS via crafted HTML input in x/net/html),
# fixed in x/net 0.62.0. In the image it is carried only by the prebuilt gh
# binary at /usr/bin/gh — the scan's sole target for this ID — so per-ID
# suppression hides nothing fixable elsewhere. gh floats from the
# cli.github.com apt repo (see the Dockerfile) rather than a pins/ entry, so
# the 7-day pin soak does not gate it; but the latest release, v2.103.3
# (2026-10-26), still vendors x/net 0.60.0 and cli/cli's trunk is at 0.61.0,
# so no gh release carries the fix yet and a bump cannot clear this today.
# grype already accepts the finding via .grype.yaml's /usr/bin/** go-module
# rule. Expiry: gh ships several releases a week (v2.103.0–v2.103.3 within
# five days of October), so one bundling >= 0.62.0 is due within days and the
# 11-16 and 11-23 Monday scans give two weekly windows to land it. The scan
# builds with cache-from gha, so the apt layer can keep serving the old gh
# deb after a fixed release exists: at expiry, check the latest gh release's
# go.mod for x/net >= 0.62.0 and, if fixed, touch the gh RUN in the Dockerfile
# to rebuild that layer, then drop this entry.
CVE-2026-78661 exp:2026-11-25
